<div class="not-prose">
<p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">If your car is gone from where you parked it in Spokane, call <strong class="text-white">Crime Check at (509) 456-2233</strong> first. It is the non-emergency police line for the Spokane area, and it can tell you whether your car was reported towed or impounded and which tow company has it, or take a stolen-vehicle report if it was not towed.</p><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-red-500/60 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><em>Note: Spokane Towing does not run an impound lot and does not hold towed vehicles. This is a plain-language guide using official City of Spokane and Washington State sources, linked below. Rules and numbers can change, so check the source page if anything is unclear.</em></div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Step 1: Find out who has your car</h2><div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6"><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Towed in the Spokane area (street, private lot, or police tow):</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">call Crime Check, (509) 456-2233. Under Washington law (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.100" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.100</a>), a tow company that impounds a vehicle from private property has to report it to the local police agency, so the police usually know where it went.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Towed from a private parking lot:</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">look at the signs at the lot entrance. In Washington, nonresidential private lots that tow within 24 hours must post signs showing when vehicles can be towed and the tow company’s name, phone number and address (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.070" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.070</a>). Take a photo of the sign.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Towed from I-90 or a state highway:</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">the Washington State Patrol says to contact the local WSP office or your local law enforcement agency to find out which tow company has the car (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsp.wa.gov/i-want-to/frequently-asked-questions/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSP FAQ</a>). On most state roads an abandoned car can be impounded after 24 hours, or right away if it is a hazard or blocking traffic or snowplows.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Check your mail.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">The tow company must mail the registered and legal owners a notice within 24 hours of an impound, with its name, address, phone number and who authorized the tow (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.110" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.110</a>).</p></div></div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Step 2: Get your car back</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Once you know which tow company has it, call them directly before going. Ask:</p><ul class="space-y-2 my-6"><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300">Their yard address and the hours you can pick up</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300">The total owed today, and how much storage is added each day</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300">Which payment methods they accept</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300">What ID and paperwork to bring (usually a photo ID plus registration or title)</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300">Whether there is a police or court hold on the vehicle</span></li></ul><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-amber-500/70 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Move fast.</p>Storage fees build up daily, and unclaimed vehicles can eventually be sold at auction.</div><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">If SPD itself is holding the vehicle</strong> (for example as evidence), the City says to call the SPD Property &amp; Evidence Facility at (509) 625-4130 to schedule an appointment and bring a government-issued photo ID (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/police/get-help/recover-property/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane: Recover Property</a>).</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">If there is a court hold:</strong> the City notes that for a 30, 60 or 90-day hold after a Driving While License Suspended 3rd Degree citation, you must pay outstanding court fees, fines and penalties before the court authorizes release.</p><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Your personal belongings</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Washington law requires the impound notice to tell you that the tow operator will store personal belongings found in the vehicle at no cost if you, as the owner or agent, come to retrieve them and sign a personal belongings storage request form before the auction date (RCW 46.55.110). If you cannot afford to redeem the car right away, ask about getting your belongings out.</p><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Step 3: If you think the tow was wrong</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">You can ask a court to decide whether the impound was proper and whether the towing and storage charges matched the posted rates (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/rcw/default.aspx?cite=46.55.120" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.120</a>):</p><ol class="space-y-4 my-6"><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">1</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">The tow company has to give you a hearing request form when you redeem, or try to redeem, the vehicle.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">2</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">The written request must reach the court within 10 days of that date.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">Miss it and you lose the right to a hearing.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">3</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">For impounds authorized by the City of Spokane, file with the <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/courts/municipal-court/towing-and-impounds/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane Municipal Court</a> clerk.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">The court’s page lists a $73 filing fee and asks for the completed form and your redemption receipt. Other impounds go to district court.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">4</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">Filing does not release the car.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">Storage keeps adding up until the hearing, so many people pay, get the car back, then file.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">5</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">If the court finds the impound was improper, the owner does not owe the towing and storage fees.</p></div></li></ol><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-red-500/60 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Bring evidence:</p>photos of where you parked, the signs (or the lack of them), your receipt, and the notice you received.</div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Need the car moved after you get it back?</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">If the car will not start after sitting in a yard, has a dead battery, or you would rather not drive it, we can connect you with a local partner tow company to take it to your home or mechanic. Online estimates use a <strong class="text-white">$140 hook fee plus $6 per mile, with a $200 minimum</strong>; see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towing-cost-spokane">Spokane towing cost</a> for examples, or the full list of <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/spokane-towing-services">Spokane towing services</a>.</p><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="divide-y divide-slate-800 border-y border-slate-800"><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How do I find out if my car was towed in Spokane?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Call Crime Check, the Spokane-area non-emergency police line, at (509) 456-2233. They can tell you whether the car was reported towed or impounded and which tow company has it, or take a stolen-vehicle report. If it was on a state highway, contact the Washington State Patrol.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Where is the Spokane impound lot?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">There is no single impound lot. Vehicles are held at the yard of the tow company that towed them. Crime Check (509-456-2233), the sign at the private lot, or the mailed impound notice will tell you which company and where. Vehicles held by SPD as evidence are handled through the SPD Property and Evidence Facility (509-625-4130, appointment required).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Does Spokane Towing have my car?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">No. Spokane Towing does not run an impound lot or hold towed vehicles. We connect people with a partner tow company for towing they request.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How long do I have to contest a tow in Washington?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Your written hearing request must reach the court within 10 days of the date you redeemed or tried to redeem the vehicle, and more than five days before any auction date (RCW 46.55.120). For City of Spokane impounds, file with the Spokane Municipal Court clerk; the court lists a $73 filing fee.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Can I get my stuff out of a towed car?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Washington law says the tow operator will store personal belongings found in the vehicle at no cost if the owner or agent comes to retrieve them and signs a personal belongings storage request form before the auction date. Call the tow company to arrange it.</p></div></div><div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-600 to-red-700 rounded-2xl p-8 text-center mt-12"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Need Your Car Moved After Pickup?</h3><p class="mb-6 text-white/90">We connect you with a local partner tow company. Price confirmed before dispatch.</p><a href="tel:+15097977999" class="bg-white text-red-600 px-8 py-4 rounded-xl hover:bg-gray-100 transition font-bold text-lg inline-block shadow-lg">Call (509) 797-7999</a></div>
</div>
@endsection
